findings.body:
Before this PR, `VariableAssignerNode` wrote the conversation variable inside `_run()`. If the DB write raised, the node failed the run, so the user saw the failure. After this PR the write happens in `ConversationVariablePersistenceLayer.on_event`, and `event_manager._notify_layers` wraps each layer call in `try/except Exception` and only logs it (`_logger.exception("Error in layer on_event...")`).

The result is that a failed `update()` (lost connection, row deleted mid-run, `ConversationVariableNotFoundError`) leaves the in-memory pool updated and the node reported as SUCCEEDED. The workflow continues and completes. Downstream nodes read the new value, and the DB keeps the old one, so the next turn reads stale data. Also, if one selector in the loop fails, the remaining selectors in that event are never written.

::code{file="api/core/app/layers/conversation_variable_persist_layer.py" lines="45-53"}

::callout{type="warning"}
This is a silent data-integrity regression, not just a logging concern. The write needs to be able to fail the run.

`WorkflowEntry.single_step_run` (`api/core/workflow/workflow_entry.py` ~L121-200) builds a node and calls `node.run()` directly. No graph engine runs and no layers are attached. The new layer is registered only in `AdvancedChatAppRunner` (below), so a Variable Assigner run from the draft debugger (`services/workflow_service.py` ~L696) updates the pool but writes nothing to `ConversationVariable`.

Before this PR the node wrote the row for `InvokeFrom.DEBUGGER` as well. The `DEBUGGER` exemption that used to skip the missing-conversation-id error was removed in v2, which shows the debugger path was considered. Users who edit a conversation variable in single-step debugging will now see it revert on the next real run.

::code{file="api/core/app/apps/advanced_chat/app_runner.py" lines="204-206"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The layer is wired only in the advanced-chat run path, so any other caller that executes assigner nodes loses persistence too.

The layer returns early when `system_variable.conversation_id` is None (lines 34-36). The old v1 node raised `VariableOperatorNodeError("conversation_id not found")` when the id was missing. The old v2 node raised `ConversationIDNotFoundError` for any non-DEBUGGER run.

With this change, an assigner that targets a conversation variable in a run without a conversation id completes successfully and the value is never saved. That is a silent loss of an error the code used to report. `ConversationIDNotFoundError` in `api/core/workflow/nodes/variable_assigner/v2/exc.py` is now unused in the codebase.

::code{file="api/core/app/layers/conversation_variable_persist_layer.py" lines="34-36"}

The original implementation used `with Session(db.engine) as session:`. The new code calls `session = Session(db.engine)` and never closes it. The session holds a connection until it is closed or garbage-collected. Every assigner write, and every `conversation_service` update, leaks a connection on the success path and on the `ConversationVariableNotFoundError` path.

The layer calls `update()` once per variable per event, so a workflow with several assigners can hold several open sessions at once. Under load this exhausts the connection pool.

::diff{file="api/services/conversation_variable_updater.py" lines="14-23"}

`test_skips_non_conversation_variables` puts an `environment.name` selector in the assigner output, but the mock pool is empty (`MockReadOnlyVariablePool()`). The layer's `variable_pool.get(selector)` returns None, which hits the `not isinstance(variable, Variable)` branch and never reaches `update`. If the `selector[0] != CONVERSATION_VARIABLE_NODE_ID` guard were deleted entirely, the test would still pass.

The test should put a real `environment` variable in the pool, so the only thing that prevents an update is the prefix check. The `test_skips_when_outputs_missing` name also doesn't match what it tests (empty `process_data`, not missing outputs).

::code{file="api/tests/unit_tests/core/app/layers/test_conversation_variable_persist_layer.py" lines="134-144"}
